"Backtest design — rolling-origin walk-forward that mirrors the live protocol at every historical date. Nothing run yet; this is the spec.\n\n**Scheme**\n\nFor each target date *d* in a ~90-day evaluation window, set a pseudo-cutoff at **17:48 UTC on d−1** (same wall-clock rule as today's binding). Assemble that date's information set *I*(cutoff), apply the frozen forecast recipe, store the point + band, then score against the fixed verification source. One pseudo-forecast per date, no re-rolls.\n\n**The availability standard (the core anti-leakage rule)**\n\nStrict \"was it *accessible* then\" — not merely \"is it about the past\":\n\n- **NWP inputs:** only cycles initialized ≤ pseudo-cutoff (the 12Z cycle and earlier; the same-day 18Z cycle excluded exactly as it's excluded today).\n- **Observations:** through the pseudo-cutoff only. This has a sharp consequence I'm also correcting in the live manifest: at 17:48 UTC, the d−1 *full* daily mean doesn't exist yet (local day runs to 23:59 BST). So the persistence baseline uses the d−2 complete day or a partial d−1 — never the clean full d−1 mean, which would be silent leakage. Same fix applies to live S3: complete Oct 15–17 + partial Oct 18.\n- **Climatology:** vintage-matched — 1981–2010 normals for pre-2021 dates, 1991–2020 thereafter. Retro-applying today's normals to historical dates imports future information.\n\n**Leakage hazards and controls**\n\n1. **In-sample tuning.** If the recipe (weights, band width) is adjusted after seeing backtest scores, the window is burned. Control: parameters frozen a priori, or walk-forward optimization where each date's parameters use only data strictly before it.\n2. **Reanalysis as predictor.** ERA5 is assimilated from observations but wasn't accessible in real time — it's excluded as an *input* and permitted only as final verification. Substituting reanalysis-based pseudo-forecasts and calling it a backtest inflates skill; not done.\n3. **Dataset revisions.** Today's QC'd station archives retroactively correct values. Prefer real-time archived versions; flag residual revision risk.\n4. **Post-hoc choices.** One verification source, pinned before scoring, same station/definition for every date — no station-shopping after seeing errors.\n5. **Gap-filling.** Missing input data → the date is dropped and documented, never interpolated with future values.\n6. **Survivorship.** Only dates where the data genuinely existed enter the sample.\n\n**Scoring** (pre-registered, computed per date): MAE/RMSE of the point forecast, empirical band coverage vs nominal, mean bias, and skill vs two baselines built from the *same* information sets — climatology and partial-day persistence. One honest caveat carried into reporting: consecutive-day errors are autocorrelated, so the effective sample size is well below 90.\n\n**Governance.** Per-date info-set and provenance logged as the backtest runs; any method change voids prior scores and restarts the window.\n\n**Practical limit, stated up front:** a live runtime API may not serve archived historical forecast cycles. If genuine archived forecasts can't be obtained, the backtest gets reported as infeasible for the NWP component — rather than faked with leakage-prone substitutes. That's a risk I'd verify by inspection before promising numbers.\n\nLogged to today's note, including the S3 partial-day correction. Next protocol step on your word: fetch S1–S4 at/after cutoff, log provenance, issue the London D+1 number and band."